      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avoca%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early settlers in Avoca were primarily loggers and farmers drawn to the Wisconsin River and the bountiful land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofavocawi.gov/?SEC=CE4B6B7A-B1B3-4BD8-BB17-4F8A986798C7"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village of Avoca grew rapidly after the railroad arrived in 1856.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofavocawi.gov/?SEC=CE4B6B7A-B1B3-4BD8-BB17-4F8A986798C7"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Avoca was officially incorporated on February 10, 1870.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofavocawi.gov/?SEC=CE4B6B7A-B1B3-4BD8-BB17-4F8A986798C7"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Avoca is still known today for the Avoca Prairie, which helped the village become a focal point for local agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofavocawi.gov/?SEC=CE4B6B7A-B1B3-4BD8-BB17-4F8A986798C7"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Avoca train depot was converted into a feed mill in 1958 and remained in use until 1997.",
